e're talking about ABC NBC  CBS or local TV over-the-air affiliates  why were people willing to carve out  this gigantic exception to the First  Amendment well I mean it's not people  it's a certain subset of the population  certainly  there they're interested in favor of  what's called the quid pro quo now it's  not just the regulator's it's not just  you know interest groups agitating for  more government some people might say  censorship others might call it  regulation or responsible reasonable  regulation but the broadcaster's  themselves were involved in the  so-called quid pro quo it's comes  literally from the 1927 radio act and in  this arrangement of 1927 radioactive  centrally created the modern FCC and the  regulation of giving out licenses that  gave you a minato what is now the FCC  but the the fact is that the government  gets to set certain licenses into the  market and it sets conditions on how  those licenses are used and if some of  the conditions abridge the First  Amendment that's a problem for  broadcasters who might want to have free  speech on the other hand if the  broadcasters are getting benefits out of  that system they might go along with it  having the license was worth a lot more  than having the right of free expression  on that yes and so that developed you  know what were some of the cases where  FCC regulators started turning the  screws on broadcasters or politicians  you know we're using the Fairness  Doctrine as a way to squelch free speech  even though it was supposed to actually  problem yeah well thankfully and quite  uniquely in this area we have a  documentary record of what happened with  activities political activities to  actually silence political opposition  under the Fairness Doctrine has to do  with a very famous Supreme Court case  called red lion vs. FCC now what  happened in red lion well red lion was a  tiny AM station in Pennsylvania that had  put I was owned by an evangelist  conservative quite conservative and